 know that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), which implies \\(\\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)\\). Therefore, we can use the identity for the tangent of a sum of angles to express \\(\\tan \\gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = \\tan (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\tan (\\alpha + \\beta) = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\csc 60^\\circ = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Let \\(x = \\tan \\alpha\\) and \\(y = \\tan \\beta\\). Then \\(xy = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Using the tangent addition formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - xy} = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{x + y}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3(x + y)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), we use the identity \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\\). However, a more straightforward approach is to use the product-to-sum identities and the given information.\n\nWe know that:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"33d32e52c496baa5579ffb8086d5fb1639c738793a7463bcbe449bb932d1903e"},"seconds":65.3839418888092,"seed":1028193855488935176,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by using the given information and trigonometric identities. We are given that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\) and \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nFirst, we use the identity for the tangent of a sum of angles to find \\(\\tan \\gamma\\):\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = \\tan (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\tan (\\alpha + \\beta) = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we substitute this into the formula:\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3(\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\). Using the identity for the cosine of a sum of angles, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = \\cos (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nWe can rewrite \\(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta\\) in terms of \\(\\cos \\gamma\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\alpha} \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\beta})}\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the identity in terms of tangents. We know that:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\cos \\gamma \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we use the identity:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nand since \\(\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) = -\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( 1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) = -\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)} = \\frac{1}{-\\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3} - 3} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2\\sqrt{3} + 3} = \\frac{3(2\\sqrt{3} + 3)}{12 - 9} = 2\\sqrt{3} + 3\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we start by using the given information and trigonometric identities. We are given that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\) and \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nFirst, we use the identity for the tangent of a sum of angles to find \\(\\tan \\gamma\\):\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = \\tan (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\tan (\\alpha + \\beta) = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we substitute this into the formula:\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3(\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\). Using the identity for the cosine of a sum of angles, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = \\cos (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nWe can rewrite \\(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta\\) in terms of \\(\\cos \\gamma\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\alpha} \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\beta})}\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the identity in terms of tangents. We know that:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\cos \\gamma \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we use the identity:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nand since \\(\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) = -\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( 1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) = -\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)} = \\frac{1}{-\\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3} - 3} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2\\sqrt{3} + 3} = \\frac{3(2\\sqrt{3} + 3)}{12 - 9} = 2\\sqrt{3} + 3\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:399"}
